The tool descriptions still said macOS
=====================================

The prompt has said `running on linux` since the platform line was written — `platform =
std::env::consts::OS` resolves correctly — but the tool catalogue did not, and the catalogue is
what the model reads when it decides how to act. Three ways it kept being told this was a Mac:

  * the computer-use `key` tool's own example was `'cmd+a'`. On Linux `cmd` is not a key, so a
    combination written that way is delivered as a literal letter and the shortcut silently does
    nothing. It says `ctrl+a` now, and says why.
  * `app_bring_to_current_space` — "Bring an application window to the current macOS Space" — was
    offered on a desktop with no Mission Control. A tool that exists teaches the model the concept
    exists. It is filtered out of the catalogue anywhere that is not macOS.
  * the Computer Use extension described itself as "Control local macOS apps…" in Tools & skills.

Nothing here is deleted from the source: the macOS strings are still the macOS strings, selected by
`cfg!(target_os = "macos")`. This is the same class of fix as `0023` (which took `open -a` and
`osascript` out of the Bash examples) and `0021` (which stopped seeding the AppleScript server) — the
third place the build was still speaking Macintosh on a Linux box.

Why it matters beyond tidiness: an agent that believes it is on macOS reaches for `osascript`,
`open -a`, `defaults write`, `pbcopy` and `/Applications` paths. Every one of those is a failed tool
call the user watches happen.
